1. What is the difference between JDK, JRE, and JVM?
link - https://www.geeksforgeeks.org/java/differences-jdk-jre-jvm/

My answer -

First, I install the JDK on my Windows operating system because it provides the tools required to develop Java applications.

When I execute `javac Hello.java`, the Java compiler compiles the source code into bytecode and generates a `Hello.class` file.

When I run `java Hello`, the JVM starts the execution process. It loads the required classes, verifies the bytecode, and performs linking and initialization.

The JVM execution engine then executes the bytecode using an interpreter. For frequently executed code, the JIT compiler can compile the bytecode into native machine code to improve performance.

Finally, the native machine instructions are executed by the processor. This process allows the same Java bytecode to run on different operating systems that have compatible JVM implementations.
